<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xu Guangqi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1562">1562</a>–<a href="/years/1633">1633</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Hsü Kuang-ch'i, Paul Siu, Doctor Paul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xu Guangqi was a Chinese agronomist, astronomer, mathematician, politician and writer of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, and one of the Three Pillars of Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, he met the Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci">Matteo Ricci</a> in 1600, was baptised as Paul in 1603, and in 1607 translated with Ricci the first parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclids-elements">Euclid's Elements</a> into Chinese. Rising to be Minister of Rites and Deputy Senior Grand Secretary, he was appointed in 1629 to lead a reform of the calendar with Jesuit help, completed after his death as the Chongzhen calendar. His Complete Treatise on Agriculture, of some 700,000 characters, was edited by Chen Zilong and published in 1639.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xu-guangqi.jpg" alt="Xu Guangqi" width="330" height="635" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:徐光啟.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Xu Guangqi was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, then a small walled county seat in Songjiang Prefecture, on 24 April 1562. His branch of the Xu clan was not related to those that had passed the imperial examinations and joined the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>. His father, Xu Sicheng, had been orphaned at five and lost most of his inheritance to pirate raids and insolvent friends. By the time Xu was six the family had saved enough to send him to a local school, and from 1569 to 1573 he attended the school at the Buddhist monastery at Longhua. His mother died on 8 May 1592, and after the mourning period he seems to have failed the provincial examination in 1594.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1596 Xu moved to Xunzhou in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> to assist its prefect, and he passed the provincial examination at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> the next year, but failed the imperial examination. He turned to military and agricultural subjects and studied under Cheng Jiasui. After meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci">Matteo Ricci</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> in 1600 he collaborated with him on translations of Western texts into Chinese and of Confucian texts into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. Ricci's influence led to his baptism in 1603. From 1607 to 1610 he was forced to retire and experimented at home with Western-style irrigation and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweet-potato">sweet potatoes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and the nu zhen tree.</p><p>In 1629 his memorials persuaded the court to let the Portuguese captain Gonçalo Teixeira-Correa bring ten artillery pieces and four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombard">bombards</a> to the capital. The guns were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dengzhou">Dengzhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, where his protégé Ignatius Sun was governor. After a mutiny under Kong Youde and Geng Zhongming captured Dengzhou in 1632, Xu's pleas for clemency failed and Sun was court-martialled and executed. He rose to be Minister of Rites and Deputy Senior Grand Secretary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Besides the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a> translation and the calendar reform, Xu wrote a book on military techniques, Mr Xu's Amateur Observations, and put forward the concept of a Rich Country and Strong Army, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> later adopted as Fukoku Kyohei. His Complete Treatise on Agriculture followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-zhen-eunuch">Wang Zhen</a> and Jia Sixie and was seven times as long as either. It treated irrigation, fertilisers, famine relief, economic crops and empirical observation. The Chongzhen calendar introduced European mathematics and astronomy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, with the Tychonic system as its theoretical basis.</p><p>Xu died on 8 November 1633. Johann Adam Schall von Bell stayed with him in his final illness and oversaw the return of his body to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, where it was buried in 1641. His tomb is the centrepiece of Guangqi Park.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xu Guangqi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late Ming the Jesuits arrived, the most influential of them Matteo Ricci, who worked with the convert <b>Xu Guangqi</b>; the Nanjing incident of 1616 led to the exile of four Jesuits to Macau.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Shuntian Fu, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.23,121.47&amp;year=1608" aria-label="Shanghai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Xu Guangqi"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5107 4894h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5107 4894h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.23,121.47&amp;year=1608">What was here in 1608? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
